<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Korean Liberation Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Korean Liberation Army was the armed force of the Provisional Government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a>, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> in September 1940 with Chinese Nationalist support. Its members fought alongside Chinese and British forces against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, including in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, and trained with the American OSS for a landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> that Japan's surrender in 1945 made unnecessary. It is regarded as a forerunner of the South Korean army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/korean-liberation-army.jpg" alt="Korean Liberation Army" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Korean Liberation Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Korean_Liberation_Army.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1910 and crushed the March First Movement of 1919, Korean exiles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> founded the Korean Provisional Government, which wanted an army from the start but lacked money and men. In 1933 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-gu">Kim Gu</a> won leave from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> to train Korean fighters at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> branch of the Republic of China Military Academy, disguised as an all-Chinese class; 62 of its 92 students graduated before it closed on 9 April 1935, and most later joined the army. The government fled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> across China after war broke out in July 1937 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> around 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chiang approved Kim's proposal on 11 April 1940 but then withdrew funding over the army's independence, and Kim went ahead. The army was proclaimed on 17 September 1940 at a dawn ceremony in the Jialing Hotel, timed to avoid Japanese air raids, with General Chi Ch'ŏngch'ŏn as commander and General Lee Beom-seok later as chief of staff. It moved its headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a> and grew to 3,600 troops by 1943, though it suffered from funding problems and infighting. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> recognised it on 28 May 1941 and placed it under the Chinese commander-in-chief, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> declined to recognise the government in exile.</p><p>In 1942 the Korean Volunteers Army of Kim Won-bong, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-gu">Kim Gu</a>'s left-leaning rival, was merged into it, to the displeasure of both sides. At British request, for Japanese speakers, nine men went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> on 29 August 1943 and served on the edges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, notably at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-imphal">Battle of Imphal</a>. On 1 May 1945 the government in exile won full control of the army, and with the Office of Strategic Services it prepared the Eagle Project, a mission into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> set to leave on 20 August under Lee. Japan's announced surrender on 15 August ended the plan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The army was disbanded in June 1946, and its members returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in late 1945 and 1946. Many of them, including Generals Chi and Lee, joined the Government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a>, and some of its generals and officers helped form the ROK Armed Forces. There has long been a campaign in South Korea to move the National Armed Forces Day from 1 October to 17 September, the army's founding day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Korean Liberation Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Kim Gu it organised attacks on Japanese leaders, including Yun Bong-gil's bombing in Shanghai in 1932, then moved through several Chinese cities before settling in Chongqing in 1940, where it formed the <b>Korean Liberation Army</b> and declared war on Japan in 1941.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-korea">Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea</a></li></ul></section>
